course: dictionary
lesson: 8388
title: Words named after people
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words come from a person's name. Rubenesque comes from the painter Peter Paul Rubens, who painted round, full figures. When a word is named after a person, the name is a clue to its meaning.
explain: Read the given sentence: «"Rubenesque" means plump and attractive, after the painter Rubens, who painted round women.» The words plump and attractive tell you the meaning, and the words after the painter Rubens tell you where it comes from.
explain: Rubenesque is a polite word, called a euphemism. It is kinder than harsher words, but it still describes a person's body. Use it only when you need it, and never to tease or comment on someone.
fact: Rubenesque is an adjective, and it can also be written with a capital R.
fact: «Rubenesque» means plump, with a full, rounded figure, like the women in Rubens's paintings.
fact: Rubenesque can also mean in the style of the painter Peter Paul Rubens.
fact: A euphemism is a polite way of saying something that could sound harsh.
fact: Context clues, like the words after the painter Rubens, can help you work out where a word comes from.
exercise: «The painting showed a rubenesque woman with a full, rounded figure.» What does rubenesque mean? | *Plump, with a full, rounded figure | Old and grey-haired | Painted in bright colours | Very thin and tall
exercise: Where does the word rubenesque come from? | *The painter Peter Paul Rubens | A famous Scottish castle | A type of ocean fish | A village in Australia
exercise: Why is rubenesque a careful word to use about a person? | It is only used in sport | *It can still feel like a comment on someone's body | It means exactly the same as tall | It is slang for happy
test: Which word in «"Rubenesque" means plump and attractive, after the painter Rubens» tells you where the word comes from? | Plump | Means | Attractive | *After the painter Rubens
test: In the style of which painter can rubenesque also mean? | Vincent van Gogh | Pablo Picasso | *Peter Paul Rubens | Leonardo da Vinci
exam: Rubenesque is a polite word, so why should you still think before using it about a person? | Because it only describes buildings | It is a rude word for cold weather | *Because it can still feel like a comment on someone's body | Because it means thin and tall
Note: the test and exam options above are fine; the exam question is the answer you need to read.
